ADR-09: Tenant Ownership and Role-Based Authorization Extension

Date
26 May 2026

Status
Accepted

Related ADRs:
- ADR-05: Authentication System Design
- ADR-06: Role-Based Access Control
- ADR-07: Permission Enforcement

Context

ADR-05 introduced authentication features including login, logout, registration, session authentication, and protected access using LoginRequiredMixin.

Although authentication was implemented successfully, the application still had an important limitation. Logged-in users were not linked to tenant records, meaning the system could not determine which user belonged to which tenant.

As a result:

- tenant ownership filtering was not possible
- repair requests could not be restricted per user
- user actions were not linked to domain entities
- future object-level permissions could not be supported
- role hierarchy only supported Maintenance Staff

To satisfy Assessment 4 authentication and authorization requirements, the authentication architecture needed to be extended into tenant ownership and role-based authorization.

Problem

Without tenant ownership:

- users could log in but remained disconnected from tenant records
- repair information could not be filtered by logged-in user
- permission boundaries were incomplete
- service layer integration for tenant filtering was not possible
- repair workflows did not fully reflect real housing operations

Alternatives Considered

Option 1: Keep authentication only

Advantages:
- simple implementation
- no model changes required
- minimal configuration

Disadvantages:
- no tenant ownership
- users disconnected from domain entities
- no object-level permission support
- limited authorization architecture


Option 2: Store tenant information inside sessions

Advantages:
- easy implementation
- avoids model changes

Disadvantages:
- duplicated information
- weak relational integrity
- difficult maintenance
- poor scalability


Option 3: Link Tenant to Django User and extend role hierarchy

Advantages:
- supports tenant ownership
- enables permission filtering
- aligns users with repair records
- supports future object-level permissions
- reflects real repair workflow

Disadvantages:
- migration changes required
- additional admin setup required

Decision

The system was extended by linking Tenant with Django User through OneToOneField:

user = models.OneToOneField(
    User,
    on_delete=models.CASCADE,
    related_name="tenant_profile",
    null=True,
    blank=True
)

This creates a direct relationship between authentication accounts and domain tenants.

The role hierarchy was also expanded:

1. Tenant
- create repair requests
- view own repair information

2. Maintenance Staff
- update repairs
- create maintenance updates

3. Housing Manager
- manage repair workflow
- access broader repair management functions

MaintenanceStaffRequiredMixin was extended to support:

- Maintenance Staff group
- Housing Manager group
- Django staff users

using:

user.groups.filter(
    name__in=[
        "Maintenance Staff",
        "Housing Manager"
    ]
).exists()

RepairRequest.get_absolute_url() was also updated to use:

repairrequest-detail

to maintain consistency with application routing.

This decision establishes the foundation for tenant-specific filtering and future object-level permission enforcement.

Code References

housing/models.py
- Tenant.user OneToOneField
- RepairRequest.get_absolute_url()

housing/views.py
- MaintenanceStaffRequiredMixin
- LoginRequiredMixin implementation

housing/admin.py
- Tenant administration configuration

Django Admin
- Groups:
   • Tenant
   • Maintenance Staff
   • Housing Manager

Consequences

Positive:
- tenant ownership supported
- stronger authorization model
- future object-level permissions enabled
- role hierarchy improved
- supports HD authentication requirements
- supports tenant filtering in service layer
- better alignment with real housing workflow

Negative:
- additional migrations required
- extra admin configuration
- user-tenant linking required
- more permission management complexity
